The doctor quickly examines me.
He checks my throat and measures my temperature.
Then he smiles kindly.
"You are not well today, but it is nothing serious," he says.
"It is only a seasonal viral infection."
"Because of the rain, many people have a cold."
I nod slowly.
"Sir, what should I eat?" I ask.
The doctor writes a prescription on a piece of paper.
He gives the paper to me.
"You should take these medicines for three days," he advises.
"You should drink plenty of warm water."
"You should eat only light food."
"And you should rest as much as possible at home."
I look at the prescription and thank him.
"I will go to the pharmacy now," I tell him.
I put the paper in my bag carefully.
I feel a little better, and I leave the clinic.
